cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
title: "datasource-foundationdb: the multiplayer-fabric fork of FoundationDB"
abstract: >
  FoundationDB, the distributed ordered key-value store with ACID transactions,
  forked so this project can own the Windows builds and carry the
  portability-consensus branch. The database is upstream's and so is its README; what
  this copy holds is that branch. A data source implements a repository, and this is
  the one every durable transaction in the fabric ultimately lands in -- datasource-queen
  and datasource-store are its callers, through a SQLite VFS whose pages live here.
authors:
  - name: "Apple Inc. and the FoundationDB project authors"
version: 0.1.0-dev.1
date-released: "2026-08-17"
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/datasource-foundationdb"
keywords:
  - foundationdb
  - distributed-database
  - acid-transactions
  - fork
references:

  - type: software
    title: "FoundationDB"
    authors:
      - name: "Apple Inc. and the FoundationDB project authors"
    url: "https://github.com/apple/foundationdb"
    notes: >
      Upstream, and GitHub carries the fork flag for this one. It tracks
      portability-consensus rather than a release branch. Owning the Windows builds is
      not owning the code, which is why the README here stays upstream's and this file
      does not.

  - type: software
    title: "datasource-queen: durable state, SQLite on a VFS whose pages live in FoundationDB"
    authors:
      - family-names: Lee
        given-names: "K. S. Ernest (iFire)"
    url: "https://github.com/v-sekai-multiplayer-fabric/datasource-queen"
    notes: >
      The consumer this fork exists for. No client opens a database and authority never
      moves, because the pages are transactions here rather than a file anybody holds.
